content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:268:4. **The BMI 40+ bullet turns an assessment indicator into an eligibility and effectiveness claim**
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:270:> Draft L102-L105: BMI 40+: almost everyone in this range is eligible for surgery and for many patients it represents the most effective long-term pathway available. Your surgeon can confirm which procedure is the right fit.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:272:> BMI 40+: MBS eligibility is assessed individually against the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility or a particular procedure; your surgeon will discuss whether surgery or another pathway is appropriate for you.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:274:Why it matters: BMI 40+ is an MBS indicator, not a promise of eligibility or comparative effectiveness.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:276:5. **The comorbidity bullet says a condition can strengthen a case regardless of BMI**
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:278:> Draft L107-L111: Weight-related health conditions or comorbidities: certain conditions strengthen a surgical case regardless of BMI. These include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH), and severe acid reflux.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:280:> Weight-related health conditions: a major obesity-related comorbidity is relevant to MBS assessment at BMI 35 or above, but a condition does not create MBS eligibility regardless of BMI. Examples may include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH) and severe acid reflux; your surgeon will assess the full clinical picture.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:284:6. **The BMI 30 FAQ repeats the qualification error**
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:286:> Draft L233-L240: **1. My BMI is around 30; am I too low for weight loss surgery?  **
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:287:> Not necessarily. A BMI of 30-34 may qualify for a surgical pathway when weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present. If surgery is not the right starting point, our medical team can offer meaningful support through medication and dietitian-led care. The only way to know where you stand is a proper assessment.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:291:Why it matters: The FAQ must answer the reader directly without implying that a comorbidity creates an MBS pathway below BMI 35.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:293:### G3 — concrete cut-list, FAQ answers and verified internal links
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:295:The cut-list is:
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:297:- **Remove publishable-page metadata:** delete the brief-only block beginning `**05_Am I Eligible for Weight Loss Surgery?**` and ending the semantic-term list at “metabolic surgery” (Draft L1-L42), including the keyword table. Start the publishable copy at “Am I eligible for weight loss surgery in Perth?”
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:298:- **Compress repeated reassurance:** replace Draft L113-L117, beginning “A history of diets that haven't worked...” and ending “You are not here because you didn't try hard enough.”, with one measured sentence: “Previous non-surgical weight-loss attempts may inform assessment, but they do not establish eligibility and are not a judgement about you.”
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:299:- **Keep the process explanation and shorten the duplicate FAQ:** retain Draft L133-L139 as the process copy, but replace Draft L266-L273, beginning “Your initial consultation runs for 30-60 minutes...” and ending “no pressure to decide anything on the day.”, with: “At your first appointment, your surgeon reviews your weight history, health conditions, goals and questions, then explains which pathways may be suitable. Costs and next steps are discussed without pressure to proceed.”
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:300:- **Replace the full cost block:** cut Draft L208-L221, including the named finance options and repeated itemised-breakdown sentence, and use the G1 replacement plus links to `/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth` and `/medicare-private-health-cover`.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:301:- **Replace the CTA scaffold:** cut Draft L223-L227 and use one patient-facing heading and one direct link: “### Find out where you stand” followed by “Book a [consultation](/book-consultation) to discuss your eligibility and options.”
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:302:- **Tighten the FAQ block while adding the missing search answers:** remove the editorial instruction at Draft L231, keep existing high-value questions to 2-3 sentences, and replace the current final FAQ block from Draft L284-L291 with the paste-ready replacement below. This adds cost in Perth, Medicare coverage, the 30/30 rule, who may not be suitable and 10-year outcomes without expanding the finance section.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:304:#### Paste-ready FAQ replacement
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:306:Replace Draft L284-L291 with:
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:310:> **8. How much does weight loss surgery cost in Perth?** There is no single Perth price. Costs vary by procedure, hospital, surgeon, anaesthetist, tests, private-health cover, excess, waiting periods and individual circumstances. Any applicable range or estimate is discussed at consultation, with an itemised breakdown before you decide.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:312:> **9. Does Medicare cover weight loss surgery?** Medicare benefits may apply when the relevant MBS item, clinical and referral requirements are met, but Medicare does not cover every cost and no rebate should be assumed. The applicable item depends on the procedure—31575 is sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 is Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and 31581 is BPD/duodenal switch—so your surgeon and insurer should confirm what applies.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:316:> **11. What can happen over 10 years after weight loss surgery?** Long-term experiences vary. Some people maintain changes, while others regain weight, develop nutritional issues or need further treatment; ongoing medical, dietary and supplement follow-up may remain important. Your surgeon can discuss the evidence and what your health and follow-up plan may mean for you, but no single 10-year outcome can be promised.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:318:The verified destination slugs available in `content-briefs/` are: `/gastric-sleeve-perth`, `/gastric-bypass-perth`, `/non-surgical-weight-loss`, `/medicare-private-health-cover`, `/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth`, `/book-consultation`, `/gp-referral`, `/faqs`, `/hospitals-locations` and `/surgeons`.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:322:> Draft L129-L131: You can contact us directly or ask your GP to send a referral. A GP referral can help with Medicare rebates on consultation fees so it is worth asking for one, but it is not required to get started.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:326:> Draft L135-L139: Your MBBS FRACS surgeon will explain which options are likely to suit you, walk through relevant procedures, and answer your questions. Costs are discussed openly so you leave knowing exactly where you stand, with no obligation to proceed.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:328:> Your bariatric surgeon will explain which options may suit you, including [gastric sleeve surgery](/gastric-sleeve-perth), [gastric bypass surgery](/gastric-bypass-perth), [non-surgical weight loss support](/non-surgical-weight-loss) or another pathway. Costs and next steps are discussed at consultation without pressure to proceed.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:330:> Draft L208-L221: The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies depending on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your hospital excess, and anaesthetist fees. For patients with appropriate private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates apply when eligibility criteria are met. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, financing options such as Zip and TLC are available, and accessing your superannuation for weight loss surgery may be possible in certain circumstances. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear, itemised breakdown of what applies to your situation. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear, itemised breakdown of every cost that applies to your situation so there are no surprises.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:332:> The [cost of weight loss surgery in Perth](/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth) varies by procedure and individual circumstances. Read our [Medicare and private health cover guide](/medicare-private-health-cover), then ask the team for an itemised estimate at consultation; coverage and out-of-pocket costs must be confirmed with the relevant providers.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:334:Why it matters: The cut-list names the exact blocks to remove or compress, and each internal link points to a real destination slug rather than a bare “here” anchor.
