docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:191:1. **The FAQ repeats an unqualified mental-health assurance and an outcome promise**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:193:> Draft L251-L256: **3. Will a mental health history stop me from being eligible?  **
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:194:> No. Psychological wellbeing is part of the assessment process, but a mental health history is not a reason to be turned away. A psychological review is there to set you up for the best possible outcome and make sure the timing and support are right for you.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:196:> **3. Will a mental health history stop me from being eligible?** A mental health history does not automatically rule out surgery. Psychological wellbeing is part of the assessment, and active or unstable concerns, an untreated eating disorder, substance misuse or difficulty following aftercare may affect timing or suitability. Your surgeon will discuss what support or further review may be needed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:198:Why it matters: The FAQ is a second occurrence after the eligibility bullet. It must not promise that a mental-health history will never affect suitability or that review will produce a particular outcome.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:202:> Draft L172-L174: Care from MBBS FRACS-certified bariatric surgeons, with procedures performed at three leading private hospital locations in Perth.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:210:> Draft L202-L204: Surgery is a powerful tool, but it works best when supported by the habits and care built around it — it is not a standalone cure.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:212:> Surgery is one treatment option with ongoing eating, nutrition and follow-up requirements. Your surgeon and care team will explain the likely benefits, limits and risks for your circumstances; outcomes vary between individuals.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:216:4. **The cost paragraph promises a significant level of private-health cover**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:218:> Draft L208-L216: The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies depending on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your hospital excess, and anaesthetist fees. For patients with appropriate private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates apply when eligibility criteria are met. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, financing options such as Zip and TLC are available, and accessing your superannuation for weight loss surgery may be possible in certain circumstances.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:220:> The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies by procedure, hospital, clinician fees, private-health policy, waiting periods, excess and individual circumstances. Medicare or private-health benefits may apply only when the relevant requirements are met; ask Medicare and your insurer to confirm what applies. Any applicable range or estimate is discussed at consultation, and the team should provide an itemised estimate without promising coverage or naming finance providers.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:224:5. **The GP-referral FAQ makes a second rebate and out-of-pocket claim**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:226:> Draft L258-L264: **4. Do I need a GP referral, or can I refer myself?  **
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:227:> You can contact us directly; a GP referral is not required to get started. That said, a referral is worth having as it allows Medicare rebates to apply to your specialist consultation fee, reducing your out-of-pocket cost for the appointment. If you don't have one yet, our team can advise you on next steps when you get in touch.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:231:Why it matters: A referral should not be presented as guaranteed rebate access or a guaranteed reduction in the patient's cost.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:233:6. **The FAQ contains a secondary GLP-1 reference**
