docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:240:Why it matters: This is the second explicit GLP-1 occurrence after the first-pass fix. The replacement answers who may not be suitable without implying that every reader will find a satisfactory pathway.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:244:1. **The summary incorrectly places BMI 30-34 on a qualification pathway**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:246:> Draft L84: As a general guide, surgical assessment is typically available for those with a BMI of ~35, and almost always from 40 and higher. Someone with a BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify where weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present and there have been previous attempts at weight loss through diet, exercise, or non-surgical means without lasting success. Please note that these are general clinical indicators only; your surgeon will make a clinical assessment and discuss your options with you when you meet.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:248:> As a general guide, MBS eligibility is assessed individually. The relevant thresholds are BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35 or above with a major obesity-related comorbidity, alongside the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; your surgeon will assess your wider health picture and discuss any other clinical pathways.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:250:Why it matters: “May qualify” is still self-qualification language, and the MBS rule does not create eligibility below BMI 35.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:252:2. **The BMI 30-34 bullet implies surgery may be appropriate under MBS**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:254:> Draft L92-L95: BMI 30-34 with a weight-related health condition: surgery may be appropriate at this range when conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present, which can strengthen the case for a surgical pathway.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:256:> BMI 30-34 with a weight-related health condition: this range does not meet the MBS threshold for these items. Your surgeon can assess your broader clinical picture and discuss non-MBS options where appropriate; a condition below BMI 35 does not by itself establish eligibility for MBS-funded surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:258:Why it matters: A major comorbidity is relevant to the MBS pathway at BMI 35+, not as a below-35 qualification route.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:260:3. **The BMI 35-39 bullet says other conditions are unnecessary**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:262:> Draft L97-L100: BMI 35-39: most people in this range are eligible for a surgical assessment, with or without other health conditions. A range of procedures may be suitable, and your surgeon will discuss the options most appropriate for your situation.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:264:> BMI 35-39: MBS eligibility is assessed individually. At BMI 35 or above, the MBS pathway requires a major obesity-related comorbidity alongside the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. Your surgeon will assess your medical history, risks, goals and aftercare readiness before discussing options.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:266:Why it matters: This is the known G2 error. BMI 35+ does not remove the major-comorbidity requirement for MBS eligibility.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:268:4. **The BMI 40+ bullet turns an assessment indicator into an eligibility and effectiveness claim**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:270:> Draft L102-L105: BMI 40+: almost everyone in this range is eligible for surgery and for many patients it represents the most effective long-term pathway available. Your surgeon can confirm which procedure is the right fit.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:272:> BMI 40+: MBS eligibility is assessed individually against the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility or a particular procedure; your surgeon will discuss whether surgery or another pathway is appropriate for you.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:274:Why it matters: BMI 40+ is an MBS indicator, not a promise of eligibility or comparative effectiveness.
