189:### G1 — residual compliance claims
191:1. **The FAQ repeats an unqualified mental-health assurance and an outcome promise**
193:> Draft L251-L256: **3. Will a mental health history stop me from being eligible?  **
200:2. **The hospital bullet uses a comparative marketing claim**
202:> Draft L172-L174: Care from MBBS FRACS-certified bariatric surgeons, with procedures performed at three leading private hospital locations in Perth.
208:3. **The risks section calls surgery a “powerful tool”**
210:> Draft L202-L204: Surgery is a powerful tool, but it works best when supported by the habits and care built around it — it is not a standalone cure.
216:4. **The cost paragraph promises a significant level of private-health cover**
218:> Draft L208-L216: The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies depending on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your hospital excess, and anaesthetist fees. For patients with appropriate private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates apply when eligibility criteria are met. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, financing options such as Zip and TLC are available, and accessing your superannuation for weight loss surgery may be possible in certain circumstances.
224:5. **The GP-referral FAQ makes a second rebate and out-of-pocket claim**
226:> Draft L258-L264: **4. Do I need a GP referral, or can I refer myself?  **
233:6. **The FAQ contains a secondary GLP-1 reference**
235:> Draft L284-L291: **7. What if I find out surgery is not right for me?  **
242:### G2 — MBS-inaccurate BMI wording
244:1. **The summary incorrectly places BMI 30-34 on a qualification pathway**
246:> Draft L84: As a general guide, surgical assessment is typically available for those with a BMI of ~35, and almost always from 40 and higher. Someone with a BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify where weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present and there have been previous attempts at weight loss through diet, exercise, or non-surgical means without lasting success. Please note that these are general clinical indicators only; your surgeon will make a clinical assessment and discuss your options with you when you meet.
252:2. **The BMI 30-34 bullet implies surgery may be appropriate under MBS**
254:> Draft L92-L95: BMI 30-34 with a weight-related health condition: surgery may be appropriate at this range when conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present, which can strengthen the case for a surgical pathway.
260:3. **The BMI 35-39 bullet says other conditions are unnecessary**
262:> Draft L97-L100: BMI 35-39: most people in this range are eligible for a surgical assessment, with or without other health conditions. A range of procedures may be suitable, and your surgeon will discuss the options most appropriate for your situation.
268:4. **The BMI 40+ bullet turns an assessment indicator into an eligibility and effectiveness claim**
270:> Draft L102-L105: BMI 40+: almost everyone in this range is eligible for surgery and for many patients it represents the most effective long-term pathway available. Your surgeon can confirm which procedure is the right fit.
276:5. **The comorbidity bullet says a condition can strengthen a case regardless of BMI**
278:> Draft L107-L111: Weight-related health conditions or comorbidities: certain conditions strengthen a surgical case regardless of BMI. These include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH), and severe acid reflux.
284:6. **The BMI 30 FAQ repeats the qualification error**
286:> Draft L233-L240: **1. My BMI is around 30; am I too low for weight loss surgery?  **
