-> The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies by procedure, hospital, clinician fees, private-health policy, waiting periods, excess and individual circumstances. Medicare or private-health benefits may apply only when the relevant requirements are met; ask Medicare and your insurer to confirm what applies. Any applicable range or estimate is discussed at consultation, and the team should provide an itemised estimate without promising coverage or naming finance providers.
+> The [cost of weight loss surgery in Perth](/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth) varies by procedure, hospital, clinician fees, private-health policy, waiting periods, excess and individual circumstances. [Medicare and private-health benefits](/medicare-private-health-cover) may apply only when the relevant requirements are met; ask Medicare and your insurer to confirm what applies. Any applicable range or estimate is discussed at consultation, and the team should provide an itemised estimate without promising coverage or naming finance providers.
 
 Why it matters: “A significant portion ... is covered” and named funding options can read as a coverage or access promise. Keep the funding explanation measured and consultation-based.
 
@@ -235,7 +219,7 @@
 > Draft L284-L291: **7. What if I find out surgery is not right for me?  **
 > You will not leave without options. If surgery is not the right fit — due to timing, preference, or for clinical reasons — we can discuss medical weight management pathways including GLP-1 medications and dietitian-led programs. Some patients start there and revisit surgery later; others find it is sufficient on its own. The goal is the right outcome for you, whatever form that takes.</span>
 
-> **7. What if I find out surgery is not right for me?** Some people are not suitable for surgery straight away, or may not be suitable at all. Reasons can include a medical condition that makes anaesthetic or surgery too risky, pregnancy, active substance misuse, an untreated eating disorder or difficulty safely following the required aftercare. Your surgeon will explain the assessment and discuss non-surgical medical weight management or dietitian-led support where appropriate. Further medication wording remains subject to the pending TGA/legal review; do not add brand names.
+> Use the canonical replacement in the G3 “Paste-ready FAQ replacement” below; do not add a second replacement for this source block.
 
 Why it matters: This is the second explicit GLP-1 occurrence after the first-pass fix. The replacement answers who may not be suitable without implying that every reader will find a satisfactory pathway.
 
@@ -245,7 +229,7 @@
 
 > Draft L84: As a general guide, surgical assessment is typically available for those with a BMI of ~35, and almost always from 40 and higher. Someone with a BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify where weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present and there have been previous attempts at weight loss through diet, exercise, or non-surgical means without lasting success. Please note that these are general clinical indicators only; your surgeon will make a clinical assessment and discuss your options with you when you meet.
 
-> As a general guide, MBS eligibility is assessed individually. The relevant thresholds are BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35 or above with a major obesity-related comorbidity, alongside the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; your surgeon will assess your wider health picture and discuss any other clinical pathways.
+> MBS eligibility is assessed individually. The relevant thresholds are BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; your surgeon will assess your wider health picture and discuss any other clinical pathways.
 
 Why it matters: “May qualify” is still self-qualification language, and the MBS rule does not create eligibility below BMI 35.
 
@@ -253,7 +237,7 @@
 
 > Draft L92-L95: BMI 30-34 with a weight-related health condition: surgery may be appropriate at this range when conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present, which can strengthen the case for a surgical pathway.
 
-> BMI 30-34 with a weight-related health condition: this range does not meet the MBS threshold for these items. Your surgeon can assess your broader clinical picture and discuss non-MBS options where appropriate; a condition below BMI 35 does not by itself establish eligibility for MBS-funded surgery.
+> BMI 30–34 with a weight-related health condition: this range does not meet the MBS threshold for these items. Your surgeon can assess your broader clinical picture and discuss non-MBS options where appropriate; a condition below BMI 35 does not by itself establish eligibility for MBS-funded surgery.
 
 Why it matters: A major comorbidity is relevant to the MBS pathway at BMI 35+, not as a below-35 qualification route.
 
@@ -261,7 +245,7 @@
 
 > Draft L97-L100: BMI 35-39: most people in this range are eligible for a surgical assessment, with or without other health conditions. A range of procedures may be suitable, and your surgeon will discuss the options most appropriate for your situation.
 
-> BMI 35-39: MBS eligibility is assessed individually. At BMI 35 or above, the MBS pathway requires a major obesity-related comorbidity alongside the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. Your surgeon will assess your medical history, risks, goals and aftercare readiness before discussing options.
+> BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35–39.9 with at least one major obesity-related condition — always subject to individual clinical assessment. Your surgeon will assess your medical history, risks, goals and aftercare readiness before discussing options.
 
 Why it matters: This is the known G2 error. BMI 35+ does not remove the major-comorbidity requirement for MBS eligibility.
 
@@ -277,7 +261,7 @@
 
 > Draft L107-L111: Weight-related health conditions or comorbidities: certain conditions strengthen a surgical case regardless of BMI. These include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH), and severe acid reflux.
 
-> Weight-related health conditions: a major obesity-related comorbidity is relevant to MBS assessment at BMI 35 or above, but a condition does not create MBS eligibility regardless of BMI. Examples may include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH) and severe acid reflux; your surgeon will assess the full clinical picture.
+> Weight-related health conditions: a major obesity-related comorbidity is relevant to MBS assessment at BMI 35–39.9, but a condition does not create MBS eligibility below BMI 35. Examples may include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH) and severe acid reflux; your surgeon will assess the full clinical picture.
 
 Why it matters: “Regardless of BMI” conflicts with the MBS threshold and should not appear on an eligibility page.
 

